WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.238
This is the Mission Configurator.

00:00:02.238 --> 00:00:09.697
You pick a destination — Mars, Venus, Mercury, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune, Pluto, or the asteroid Ceres.

00:00:09.697 --> 00:00:23.531
The screen draws a chart called a porkchop plot , and the chart tells you every possible launch date for that destination, paired with every possible arrival date, with a cost number for each combination.

00:00:23.531 --> 00:00:24.752
Look at the chart.

00:00:24.752 --> 00:00:27.396
The horizontal axis is the launch date.

00:00:27.396 --> 00:00:29.973
The vertical axis is the arrival date.

00:00:29.973 --> 00:00:37.839
The colored contour lines are constant energy — each one represents a trajectory that costs the same amount of fuel.

00:00:37.839 --> 00:00:41.637
At the bottom of the C-shape, the contours are cheapest.

00:00:41.637 --> 00:00:44.485
That's where the spacecraft want to leave.

00:00:44.485 --> 00:00:46.384
Click anywhere on the chart.

00:00:46.384 --> 00:01:02.387
The right panel tells you exactly what that launch-and-arrival combination would mean — departure date, arrival date, transit time, departure C three in kilometers squared per second squared, arrival V infinity in kilometers per second.

00:01:02.387 --> 00:01:04.693
Notice how the cheap window moves.

00:01:04.693 --> 00:01:12.492
Mars's cheap window appears once every twenty-six months — every time Earth and Mars line up in the right geometry.

00:01:12.492 --> 00:01:17.306
Drag the time slider above the chart to walk forward five or ten years.

00:01:17.306 --> 00:01:26.596
You'll see the C-shape recur — same shape, different dates, slightly different energy values because Mars's orbit isn't a perfect circle.

00:01:26.596 --> 00:01:28.495
Try a different destination.

00:01:28.495 --> 00:01:29.716
Switch to Jupiter.

00:01:29.716 --> 00:01:37.786
Notice that the cheap window is rarer — about thirteen months apart — and the transit time is much longer, often years.

00:01:37.786 --> 00:01:39.006
Switch to Mercury.

00:01:39.006 --> 00:01:49.381
Notice that the chart looks completely different — Mercury is close, but it moves so fast around the Sun that catching it is its own engineering problem.

00:01:49.381 --> 00:01:56.976
When you find a point on the chart you like, click the Fly this mission button at the bottom of the right panel.

00:01:56.976 --> 00:02:07.894
The configurator hands you off to the Fly screen, where you watch the trajectory you just picked play out in heliocentric coordinates against the moving planets.

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:10.336
Real missions plan exactly this way.

00:02:10.336 --> 00:02:26.407
Every Mars rover, every Venus probe, every Galileo and Cassini and Voyager — somebody ran a porkchop plot first, picked the cheap window in the bottom of the C, and the rocket left the launch pad on a date the solar system had agreed to.

00:02:26.407 --> 00:02:27.696
Pick a destination.

00:02:27.696 --> 00:02:28.781
Click the chart.

00:02:28.781 --> 00:02:30.408
Argue with the geometry.

